# Content-addressed prompt files: never translate line endings.
#
# STEP-06 D1 names each file in prompts/ by the SHA-256 of its own content, and
# the point of that is that a reviewer can check the name with sha256sum. With
# core.autocrlf=true (the Windows default, and Saif's setting), a fresh checkout
# rewrites LF to CRLF, the bytes change, and sha256sum stops agreeing with the
# file name on exactly the platform this project is developed on. Measured
# during D1 by exporting the index with `git checkout-index` rather than assumed:
# all four files came back with CRLF and none matched its own name by raw bytes.
#
# The registry itself survives that, because `Path.read_text` normalizes
# newlines on read and the digest is taken over the normalized text. So this is
# not what keeps the registry loading; it is what keeps the *independent* check
# honest, and an independent check that only works on one platform is not one.
prompts/*.txt -text
prompts/manifest.json -text

# The same rule for the same reason, one directory over. STEP-08 D1's
# prompt-eval example carries its own registry (a copy of the fleet's prompts
# plus one deliberately degraded candidate), and those files are content-
# addressed by exactly the same scheme. Caught while staging the examples: git
# reported it would convert all six on checkout, which is the STEP-06 defect
# reappearing in a new location rather than a new defect.
examples/registries/**/*.txt -text
examples/registries/**/manifest.json -text
